In a VMware environment, storage performance is heavily influenced by how ESXi schedules and paths I/O requests down to your SAN fabric. When managing write-heavy, highly concurrent data engines like PostgreSQL, understanding how the underlying virtualization scheduler handles I/O is critical.How the ESXi Storage Layer Impacts Disk I/O PerformanceWhen a PostgreSQL instance running inside a VM initiates a read or write operation, it passes through multiple layers before ever hitting physical disk:The Guest OS Kernel (Postgres backend issues data page I/O).The Virtual SCSI Controller (e.g., VMware Paravirtual SCSI or Virtual NVMe).The ESXi Kernel Storage Stack (PSA - Pluggable Storage Architecture).The Multipathing Plugin (NMP or HPP).The ESXi scheduler impacts performance in two major ways:Queue Depth Congestion: If the virtual or physical queue depths are too shallow, heavy database operations (like background checkpoints or sequential table scans) will fill up the queues instantly, causing I/O commands to wait at the virtualization boundary and driving up host-side latency.Path Serialization: By default, ESXi sticks to one physical line (HBA or iSCSI port) for long durations before switching paths, leaving extra physical bandwidth unused while saturating a single link.The Storage Multipathing Configuration ModesESXi manages path scheduling using Path Selection Policies (PSP) via its Native Multipathing Plugin (NMP) or High Performance Plug-in (HPP). There are 3 main configuration modes:  Configuration Mode (PSP)How It WorksFixed (VMW_PSP_FIXED)The ESXi host designates one preferred physical path to the SAN. It will send all traffic down this single line. It only switches to an alternate path if the primary link goes completely dead.Most Recently Used (VMW_PSP_MRU)The host chooses whichever path was last successfully used to reach the storage target. Like Fixed, it does not distribute load; it sticks to a single path until a connection failure forces a swap.Round Robin (VMW_PSP_RR)The host alternates I/O requests across all healthy physical paths connected to the SAN array. This acts as a path load balancer, spreading the database I/O burden over multiple active physical ports and fabric lines.What is Best for a PostgreSQL Workload?For a high-throughput, multi-terabyte PostgreSQL production environment, Round Robin (VMW_PSP_RR) is the absolute baseline requirement. However, the default ESXi settings for Round Robin are terrible for a database.The Problem with ESXi DefaultsBy default, ESXi stays on a single path until it transmits 1,000 I/O operations or 10MB of data before switching to the next physical path.For an intensive engine like PostgreSQL, this means a heavy query or a checkpointer flushing dirty buffers will completely saturate one HBA path while your other physical paths sit entirely idle.The Golden Tuning: Switch Path at IOPS = 1To maximize your database throughput and drop storage latency by up to 50–80%, you should adjust the Round Robin configuration to switch paths after every single I/O operation (IOPS=1). This ensures true interleaving across all physical connections to the SAN.You can verify your current device configuration via the ESXi terminal using:Bashesxcli storage nmp device list -d naa.<your_device_id>
To optimize the device for your database volume, run this command at the ESXi host terminal to force it to rotate paths at every 1 IO operation:Bashesxcli storage nmp psp roundrobin device config set --type=iops --iops=1 --device=naa.<your_device_id>
Additional Critical Checklist for Postgres VMsUse the PVSCSI / vNVMe Controller: Ensure the virtual storage adapter for your Postgres data and WAL disks is set to VMware Paravirtual SCSI (PVSCSI) or Virtual NVMe, never LSI Logic SAS. PVSCSI has significantly deeper queue processing limits designed specifically for high-throughput enterprise database engines.Check Storage Array Native Features: If your SAN supports the newer VMware High Performance Plug-in (HPP) available in vSphere 7.0/8.0 (often used with NVMe-over-Fabrics), verify if your storage vendor recommends the Latency-Based Path Selection Scheme (VMW_PSP_LB) instead of Round Robin.For standard Fibre Channel/iSCSI SAN arrays, tuning Round Robin with an IOPS limit of 1 will give Postgres the widest lane possible through the hypervisor layer.
